      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Mani's Early Life</strong><br>\nMani was born in 216 AD in the Parthian Empire, which is present-day Iran. He was raised in a religious sect known as the Elcesaites, which was part of the larger Jewish Christian movement. His upbringing in this environment, which was already a blend of different religious traditions, heavily influenced his later works and the creation of Manichaeism. Mani's parents were likely of noble descent, and this may have helped him gain access to scholarly works and learning, which was rare at that time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Formation of Manichaeism</strong><br>\nAround the age of 24, Mani started preaching a new faith that was a syncretic mix of Christianity, Zoroastrianism, and Buddhism. He claimed to be the Paraclete, as promised by Jesus, and aimed to bring together the great religions of his time into a single, more universal one. This new religion, Manichaeism, emphasized dualism, the struggle between good and evil, and the importance of individual spiritual struggle.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mani's Teachings</strong><br>\nMani taught about the cosmic struggle between a good, spiritual world of light, and an evil, material world of darkness. Humans, he believed, were a mix of light and dark, and salvation could be achieved through living a life that released the light from within oneself. This meant following a strict ethical code and engaging in rituals designed to aid this process. His teachings were compiled into the 'Seven Treasures,' his holy writ, and he also created detailed paintings to accompany his teachings.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
