      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Manichaeism's Spread</strong><br>\nManichaeism spread widely during Mani's lifetime and after his death, extending through the Roman Empire, into North Africa, and East to China. The faith appealed to many because of its emphasis on an ethical lifestyle, personal salvation, and the promise of an afterlife. It flourished between the 3rd and 7th centuries AD and was at one time one of the most widespread religions in the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Persecution by Authorities</strong><br>\nDespite its initial spread, Manichaeism faced persecution from various authorities. Roman Emperors, such as Diocletian, viewed it as a threat to the established state religion and issued edicts against it. Zoroastrian priests in Persia also saw Manichaeism as heretical and persecuted its followers. This pushback was a significant factor in the religion's eventual decline.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mani's Imprisonment and Death</strong><br>\nMani was eventually imprisoned by the Persian Emperor Bahram I, at the instigation of the Zoroastrian clergy who felt threatened by the growth of Manichaeism. He died in custody in 274 AD. According to some accounts, his death was the result of either torture or execution. However, details about his death are scarce and often contain elements of legend or religious propaganda.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
