          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Artistic Contributions</strong><br>\nIn addition to his religious texts, Mani was also known as an artist and often used illustrations as a means of spreading his message. His work included complex drawings that depicted the Manichaean cosmology and mythos, playing a crucial role in educating a largely illiterate population about the tenets of his religion through visual storytelling.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Influence</strong><br>\nAlthough Manichaeism itself diminished after the rise of the Islamic Caliphate, its influence persisted in other forms. Some of Mani's concepts lived on through other religious and philosophical systems, and his emphasis on an ascetic lifestyle and the pursuit of knowledge had a lasting impact on Christian and Gnostic thought.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Rediscovery</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Mani and his teachings have seen a resurgence in interest with modern archaeological discoveries, such as the finding of the Coptic Manichaean texts in Egypt. As scholars piece together these remnants, Mani's role as a spiritual leader and his attempts to bridge different faiths continue to be recognized as significant to the understanding of religious history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
